From 81add553ab70e17c71e888bceb3576957a494a81 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 05:21:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ActiveAE: keep an NVIDIA card at full speed while HD audio
 plays through it (off by default)

On a hybrid laptop on its charger the HD-audio function of the NVIDIA GPU
reports FIFO errors ("stream N dma error: 0x28") while TrueHD or DTS-HD
plays through in 8 channels at 192 kHz, and the receiver cuts the sound;
on battery, where the card stays in one performance state, there are none
(LibreELEC Hades issue 8). The DMA of the audio function reads the samples
through the PCIe link of the card, which changes with the clocks.

New setting audiooutput.hadesgpuhold, "Keep the graphics card at full speed
while HD audio plays through" (Advanced level, shown only with an NVIDIA
card on the NVIDIA driver, off by default; strings in en_gb and Spanish).
When a sink opens for TrueHD, DTS-HD or E-AC-3 passthrough on the audio
function of an NVIDIA GPU, NVML locks the memory and graphics clocks of
that card at the highest it lists as supported
(nvmlDeviceSetMemoryLockedClocks, nvmlDeviceSetGpuLockedClocks), and
resets them when the sink closes, the sound stops or Kodi exits. A card
that refuses is left as it was and kodi.log says what each call answered;
the NVML work runs on a thread of its own, so playback never waits for it.
A marker in /run lets the next start of Kodi release a lock that a crash
left. With the setting off nothing of this runs but that one check.

The policy, the sysfs lookups and the thread are tested on the host
(TestGpuClockHold.cpp); the NVML calls were run against a stand-in library.
Not tried on a card.
